# Astro MASK of udev's predictable-net-naming rule (empty file shadows
# /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/80-net-name-slot.rules, udev(7) precedence —
# same mechanism as the resolv.conf tmpfiles override).
#
# Interfaces keep their KERNEL names (eth0, wlan0). The docs/07 network
# model is written in kernel names: the dhcpcd fallback config allowlists
# eth*, the store's ethernet map and the WAN policy speak eth0, and the
# API surfaces those names to operators. Predictable slot names exist to
# disambiguate multi-NIC servers across reboots; on fixed embedded
# hardware they only break the model — found live on qemu-x86_64 (M3
# phase 3): the PCI virtio NIC was renamed to enp0s2, no dhcpcd config
# matched it, the board booted with no addresses and the astrod-api CI
# gate could never reach SSH. arm -M virt boards were immune by accident
# (mmio virtio has no slot identity, ID_NET_NAME_SLOT stays unset).
